<Base title="About Ripples | Why useful interactions matter" description="Why Ben Khodeir started Ripples and the principles behind its people, questions, conversations and ideas."><section class="page-head about-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">About Ripples</p><h1>A useful interaction rarely <em>ends where it started.</em></h1></div><Network/></section><article class="about-copy"><h2>The idea is simple.</h2><p>Interesting people do not only exist on stages, in executive roles or behind impressive job titles. Useful knowledge is distributed much more widely than that.</p><p>Ripples exists to help people find one another through what they can give, what they are curious about and what they are willing to learn.</p><h2>Questions are welcome here.</h2><p>Good questions can be more valuable than pretending to have answers. Useful disagreement can sharpen an idea. Changing your mind can be evidence that the conversation worked.</p><h2>One thing leads to another.</h2><p>An answer can become an introduction. An introduction can become a project. A conversation can influence somebody who was not in the room. Helping someone can create consequences you never see.</p><p>Ripples is interested in those connections.</p></article><section class="philosophy"><p class="eyebrow">The philosophy</p><div><h2>Help because you can.</h2><h2>Make the next interaction count.</h2><h2>Act with integrity.</h2><h2>Trust the ripples.</h2></div></section><section class="founder"><p class="eyebrow">Founder and host</p><h2>Ben Khodeir</h2><p>Ben is a commercially minded, relationship-driven operator who is interested in systems, behaviour, practical ideas and how people think. Ripples is the structure around a habit he values: follow curiosity, make useful connections and pass on what is worth sharing.</p></section></Base>
